{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:6a4a1db45348f0147743efdafff50f0ee268abfef4c83f6aa5183c3092500517","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.5},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLVING SERIES. Conjunct 1: no official \"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE\" exists. The nearest published object is OECD's CPI excluding food and energy for the OECD total, aggregated with household-final-consumption PPP weights, NOT GDP weights, and it includes ALL 38 members. The \"/PCE\" option lets US core PCE substitute for US core CPI; core PCE has run ~0.2-0.4 pp below core CPI structurally (rent weight, chain vs Laspeyres, medical pricing source), and the US is ~52% of any PPP-GDP-weighted OECD-38 aggregate - so the CPI/PCE fork alone is worth ~0.1-0.2 pp on the aggregate and ~5 pp on the probability, toward NO.\nTHE DOMINANT ARTEFACT (toward YES). GDP weighting OECD-38 at PPP puts Turkey at ~3.5-4%, Mexico ~3.5%, Poland ~2%, Colombia/Chile/Costa Rica/Hungary ~2.5% combined. Turkey alone, at even a 15%/yr average core print over 2027-2036 (it has not been below 8% in a decade), contributes ~+0.55 pp to the aggregate; at 25% it contributes ~+0.95 pp. The OECD aggregate CPI ran roughly double its ex-Turkey counterpart through 2022-2024 for this reason. A Germany/US/Japan-weighted mental model says ~2.1-2.3%; the literal OECD-38 GDP-weighted construction says ~3.0-3.4%. This single index-construction fact moves conjunct 1 from ~0.45 to ~0.88.\nCONJUNCT 1 MARGINAL: construction-weighted. P(resolver uses the literal all-38 aggregate) 0.70, under which P = 0.88 -> 0.616. P(resolver silently uses an advanced-economy / G7-ish / ex-high-inflation construction) 0.30, under which P = 0.45 -> 0.135. Total 0.751 -> 0.75.\nCONJUNCT 2: China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr average 2027-2036, NBS monthly CPI. Observed base: China headline CPI averaged well under 1% across 2023-2026, with outright negative prints. Structural supports for low prints are also MEASUREMENT facts: the NBS basket treats owner-occupied housing through a thin rental imputation (so the property cycle barely enters CPI), the basket is reweighted every five years with weight-shift effects that have historically damped the index, and food (pork cycle) is the main upside tail. Endogenous demographics reinforce: -60M population over 2026-2035, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8, precautionary saving, excess manufacturing capacity exported as disinflation. A 10-year mean is forgiving - one 4% food-shock year adds only 0.25 pp to the mean. Risks to YES: a decisive reflation/fiscal-transfer pivot, RMB depreciation import channel, or a basket revision that adds services/housing. MARGINAL 0.72.\nCORRELATION. Slightly NEGATIVE, and the channel is explicit: Chinese deflation is exported into OECD goods core, so the very force that satisfies conjunct 2 shaves OECD core. Offsetting positive channel: both are symptoms of the same bifurcated regime. Net factor ~0.95. JOINT = 0.75 x 0.72 x 0.95 = 0.513 -> 50.\nThis is deliberately far above a naive conjunction read, and the gap is almost entirely index construction, not macroeconomics - which is precisely my lens's claim.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.398Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the Structure of Earnings Survey 2026 wave (median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, reference year 2026) and the F+Q vs J+K+M median gap moves by less than 2 pp versus SES 2022 - or Eurostat/the OECD confirms no sectoral real median hourly wage aggregate will be constructed for OECD-38.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030 (SES 2026 microdata/standard tables release)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 falls from 12 to 5-7: it confirms both that the composition drag neutralizes the F+Q advantage and that the 2036 endpoint is unobservable. If instead the gap has already opened by >= 4 pp in four years, CQ1 rises to 20-24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published CPI-excluding-food-and-energy aggregate for the OECD total: whether the OECD continues to publish the all-38 aggregate including Turkey, or introduces/promotes an 'OECD excluding high-inflation members' or 'advanced-economy' core aggregate as the headline object.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If an ex-high-inflation core aggregate becomes the standard published series, CQ2 falls from 50 to ~32 and CQ6 shifts C 28 -> 17, D 11 -> 18, B 50 -> 54. If the all-38 aggregate remains the only published core, CQ2 rises to ~56 and C holds at 28-31. This is the largest single resolution-mechanics lever in my whole payload.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS OES annual median hourly wage for NAICS 62 (health care and social assistance) relative to the all-industry median, plus the share of NAICS 62 employment in the three lowest-paid occupational groups (home health/personal care aides, nursing assistants).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031 (OES reference year 2030)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the aide share of NAICS 62 employment has risen >= 3 pp while the NAICS 62 median lags the all-industry median, my CQ1 composition artefact is confirmed and CQ1 drops to 8. If the aide share is flat and the NAICS 62 median outpaces by >= 1 pp/yr, CQ1 rises to 18-20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative OECD-38 + China strict operational data-centre electricity consumption reported by IEA/national regulators, together with FERC/ENTSO-E/CEC interconnection queue clearance times and HV transformer lead times.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If DC consumption is tracking above ~700 TWh by 2031 while interconnection queue times and transformer lead times are FALLING, the weakest-link cap is not binding: CQ3 P50 rises 1.85 -> 2.10, P90 2.65 -> 3.00, CQ6-A 11 -> 17. If DC consumption is tracking above 700 TWh while queue times lengthen and electrician wage growth accelerates, CQ3 P50 is unchanged but CQ6-C rises 28 -> 34 and CQ1's second conjunct gains ~4 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The first MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 20% after Jan 1 2027, and the sign and magnitude of the change in G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields between the equity peak and trough dates; plus the trailing 24-month average MOVE at that point.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If yields RISE or fall by less than 50 bp through that drawdown, CQ5 rises from 26 to ~45-50 (conjunct 2 largely satisfied, and the episode lifts the MOVE average). If yields fall >= 100 bp in a clean flight-to-quality and trailing MOVE is below 90, CQ5 falls to ~14 and CQ4's P10 drops toward 0.6.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'growth'."}}